  <h2>The network in two minutes</h2>
  <p>
    Public transport in Cluj-Napoca is run by <strong>CTP Cluj-Napoca</strong> and covers the
    city with buses, trolleybuses and trams, plus metropolitan lines to surrounding communes
    and a small set of night lines. Vehicles are frequent on the main corridors during the day.
    Routes and live timetables are on the official site:
    <a href="https://ctpcj.ro/index.php/en/timetables/urban-lines" rel="noopener">urban lines</a>,
    <a href="https://ctpcj.ro/index.php/en/timetables/metropolitan-lines" rel="noopener">metropolitan
    lines</a> and
    <a href="https://ctpcj.ro/index.php/en/timetables/night-lines" rel="noopener">night lines</a>.
    Google Maps also handles Cluj transit directions well for day-to-day use.
  </p>

  <h2>Tickets, and how locals actually buy them</h2>
  <p>
    The habit to copy: <strong>SMS tickets to 7479</strong>. Per
    <a href="https://ctpcj.ro/index.php/en/fares/bilete-prin-sms" rel="noopener">CTP's official
    SMS page</a>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Text the <strong>line number</strong> (e.g. "5") → a 45-minute ticket for that line: 0.72&nbsp;EUR + VAT</li>
    <li>Text <strong>T</strong> → 60 minutes on all lines: 1.40&nbsp;EUR + VAT</li>
    <li>Text <strong>A</strong> → 24 hours on all lines: 4.65&nbsp;EUR + VAT</li>
    <li>Text <strong>25N</strong> → a ticket for the night line: 1.20&nbsp;EUR + VAT</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The confirmation SMS arrives within seconds and is your ticket. One big caveat:
    <strong>this only works from a Romanian mobile number</strong>, so it pairs naturally with
    getting <a href="/cluj/guides/romanian-sim-card-foreigners/">a Romanian SIM in your first
    days</a>. Until then, use ticket machines and kiosks, or check the current paper and
    electronic options on <a href="https://ctpcj.ro/index.php/en/fares/urban-transportation" rel="noopener">CTP's
    fares pages</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>The mistake that costs money:</strong> riding with a ticket you didn't validate,
      or with a single-line SMS ticket bought for the wrong line number. Inspectors do check,
      and "I'm new here" won't get you out of a fine. When unsure, the all-lines
      <strong>T</strong> ticket is the safe buy.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Passes and student discounts</h2>
  <p>
    If you ride daily, period passes beat single tickets quickly. CTP publishes current pass
    prices on its <a href="https://ctpcj.ro/index.php/en/fares/urban-transportation" rel="noopener">urban
    fares pages</a>, and maintains a dedicated
    <a href="https://ctpcj.ro/index.php/en/" rel="noopener">discounted subscriptions</a> category
    for eligible groups, including students. Eligibility rules and required documents (typically
    proof of enrollment at a Cluj university plus ID) are set by CTP and the city and can change
    between academic years, so verify the current conditions on the official page or at a CTP
    ticket office before counting on a discount. Students: this is worth sorting in week one;
    it's one of the easiest wins in your <a href="/cluj/student-support/">Cluj setup</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Riding: the unwritten rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Board any door, validate immediately, keep the ticket until you're off.</li>
    <li>Rush hours (roughly 7–9 and 16–18) are packed on main corridors.</li>
    <li>Have your ticket ready when controllers board, they're in plain clothes.</li>
    <li>The airport is on the network too: lines A1E, 5 and 8, details in our
        <a href="/cluj/guides/cluj-airport-to-city-centre/">airport arrival guide</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When something doesn't add up</h2>
  <p>
    A machine that won't take your card, a line that vanished into a detour, a pass office
    asking for a document you've never heard of: Cluj transport is easy 95% of the time and
    baffling the other 5%. For the 5%, <a href="/cluj/foreigner-support/">ask someone local</a>:
    we answer on WhatsApp, usually within a few hours.
  </p>
